<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nannaya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Nannaya Bhattaraka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nannaya was the court poet of the Eastern Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-narendra">Rajaraja Narendra</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajahmundry">Rajahmundry</a> in the 11th century. He began the translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> into Telugu, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-mahabharatam">Andhra Mahabharatam</a>, completing about two and a half of its eighteen books, which were later finished by Tikkana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/errana">Errana</a>. He is called the Adikavi, the first poet of Telugu literature.</p></div>
